e  all of which are stimulus is that  trigger growth and hypertrophy so when  you power left and you left a very heavy  weight you're increasing your mind and  muscle connection you're increasing your  muscle fiber recruitment you're causing  micro tears and causing your muscle  fiber to come back thicker and stronger  but in bodybuilding when you train for  higher rep ranges what you're primarily  doing is building up metabolites pooling  the blood inside the muscle this is what  creates that burn sensation and this is  a stimulus of a different kind of growth  which some people refer to as  sarcoplasmic hypertrophy and this builds  the sarcoplasm the muscular equivalent  of cytoplasm in the cells which is a  kind of fluid the stuff floats around it  so this is why people think that power  lifters build a lot more strength than  body builders and it's certainly true  because simply put a power lifter lifts  a heavier weight than a bodybuilder on a  more frequent basis and so body builders  are bill  a slightly different kind of strength  not quite the same amount of explosive  strength but don't get it twisted  because a bodybuilder is still training  with very heavy weights on a very  regular basis they're still massively  increasing their mind to muscle  connection they're still massively  increasing their muscle fiber  recruitment they're building starting  strength explosive strength they are  creating more fast twitch muscle fiber  they're improving their technique when  it comes to lifting simply put a few  lift weights five times a week an hour  at a time you're gonna be stronger it  doesn't matter whether you're doing  bodybuilding or whether you're doing  powerlifting you're gonna be stronger at  one point Franco Columbu was thought to  be the strongest man alive he used  classic bodybuilding techniques you  might think the body will have fake  muscle but I challenge you to go up to a  huge bodybuilder in the gym and charge  him to an arm wrestle and see h